# 📈 Seaborn Practice

Hands-on practice notebooks covering data visualization using Seaborn, built on top of the `tips` dataset (`tipss.csv`).

## 📁 Files
- `Scatter_plot.ipynb` — Scatter plots with `hue`, `style`, and custom markers
- `bar_plotttt.ipynb` — Bar plots comparing categorical vs numeric columns (`day`, `smoker`, `time` vs `total_bill`/`tip`)
- `count_plottt.ipynb` — Count plots with `hue` and custom `palette`/`width`
- `Heatmappp.ipynb` — Correlation heatmaps with `annot`, `fmt`, and custom `linewidths`/`linecolor`
- `lineplot.ipynb` — Line plots for trend visualization, including multi-column DataFrames
- `pair__plotttt.ipynb` — Pairwise relationships across numeric variables using `pairplot()`, with `vars`, `x_vars`, `y_vars`
- `tipss.csv` — Sample restaurant tips dataset used across the notebooks

## 🧠 Concepts Covered
- **Scatter Plot** — `sns.scatterplot()` with `hue`, `style`, and `markers` for multi-dimensional comparisons
- **Bar Plot** — `sns.barplot()` for comparing averages across categories
- **Count Plot** — `sns.countplot()` for frequency of categorical values, grouped by `hue`
- **Heatmap** — `sns.heatmap()` for visualizing correlation matrices (`df.corr()`)
- **Line Plot** — `sns.lineplot()` for continuous/trend data
- **Pair Plot** — `sns.pairplot()` for exploring pairwise relationships across all numeric columns at once

## 🔑 Key Takeaway
`pairplot()` was the most useful for quick exploratory data analysis — it visualizes relationships between every pair of numeric variables in a single figure, making it easy to spot correlations and patterns before diving into individual plots.

## ⚙️ Requirements
```bash
pip install seaborn pandas matplotlib
```

## ▶️ Usage
Open any notebook in Jupyter and run the cells top to bottom. Make sure `tipss.csv` is in the same directory (or update the file path accordingly) before loading the dataset.
